 # Changelog
 
+## alpha2.3-r5 — first authenticated leg establishes the server core
+
+- Removed the R4 fixed 10-second secondary-before-primary ordering grace. Ordering is no longer a prerequisite for correctness.
+- After SMP3 HMAC, replay-nonce, leg-ID, destination, and session-ID validation, either leg 0 or leg 1 may atomically create the logical server session when no matching session exists.
+- Session creation is serialized by the server session-map mutex. The creator installs its transport leg before publishing the session, so a concurrent arrival can only JOIN a fully initialized core; exactly one routed destination connection is created.
+- Session creator and scheduling role are deliberately separate: leg0 remains preferred when present and the client still controls lazy leg1 activation. A leg1-created session is not promoted to preferred path.
+- The core already supports operation with only leg1 present; when leg0 later JOINs, new inactive/preferred traffic naturally returns to leg0 while already-outstanding frames retain normal ACK/retransmit behavior.
+- True duplicate same-ID legs remain rejected because wire v4 has no leg-generation counter.
+- Added regression tests for secondary-first creation, concurrent leg0/leg1 first arrival converging on one session, and leg1-only core data transfer.
+- SMP3 wire/hello version remains `4`; no configuration schema change is required.
+
 ## alpha2.3-r4 — server secondary-HELLO ordering fix
 
 - Live client/server logs proved that public Hy2/Snell leg1 can reach the landing SMP3 listener before the preferred-line leg0 has registered the logical session. The old server rejected such an authenticated secondary immediately as an orphan, producing a false zero-useful EOF and eventually adaptive fallback.
